/bin/zsh -lc "sed -n '801,1200p' editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-006/review-editorial-prompt.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
"I did. I had hoped that I might be excepted from that one as well. I find that I am not." There was the very least movement at the corner of his mouth. "One man walking behind another is a thing that is seen. Two are a street. I want a second pair of feet, and I had thought to ask for hers."

"I can walk," said Lira.

"You can run, which is what I am afraid of; and you have a shoulder the colour of a plum."

"Then it's me," said Cael.

He had not known that he would say it until it was out. He had been looked at for nine days, from a doorway and a kerb and the head of a stair, by people who had been told what to look for; and he found that he wished, once, to do the looking.

"It is you whom they came to see," said Seln. "I have weighed that. If he turns and finds you behind him, he will have seen a porter walking in a street at night, which is no more than he has seen already." He got up off the rung. "You will open nothing."

"Nobody opens anything."

"I say it because it will be dark, and I know what you carry that likes the dark."

---

They went out by the door where the muck went.

It was at the stable's far end, a low thing on leather hinges, and it gave upon a lane no wider than a barrow. They passed the end stall to reach it. There was a long shape in the straw there, under two blankets, with a bound leg laid out along a plank; and its eyes were open.

The old man did not speak; he looked at the two of them, and at the door, and then one raw hand came out from under the blanket and shut, slowly, into a fist.

It was the second of the four hands that a road had taught him. *Seen.*

"He learns," said Seln, in the lane, very low. "I had not been sure that he would."

They stood at the lane's mouth, a house's length from the carriage-arch, and Cael was taught the trade in three sentences.

"You will wish to look at him. Do not. Look at the place where he will be in twenty strides, and let him walk into it." The small grey man did not turn his head while he talked. "Never be the nearest thing that moves. And when he stops, you do not stop. A man who halts when another halts has signed his name; you go by, and give him to me, and when I go by I give him back."

"And if he looks round?"

"Then you are going home. You have been going home the whole time."

That made two people whom Seln had ever taught, and Cael did not say so. He stood in the mouth of a muck-lane and looked along the house-front, at the round of yellow light under a painted door's lamp.

There was a man in it.

He stood on the step, where a person stands who wishes to be seen, beside a book on a stand that he had not written in. His hood was up, and he wore a long coat of no colour. Cael looked at the boots, since he had been told of them four times: a horseman's, cut high, and at each heel a square of paler leather where a spur's rest had been taken off with a knife.

*Away?* said the hiss in his own feet. It was not a thing that Wind asked often, standing still.

He told it no. And at his knees, where it had stood since the lane under the wall, the other thing lifted its head.

Shadow had come up at dusk, as it came every dusk, and he had told it no in a lane while his eyes ran. It had not lain down; it was full night now, in a town of doorways, and he was going to follow a man through it. There was a seal on that thing, and a reason for the seal which had not altered since Ostrand; and he had never felt it lean so against a shut door.

The man under the lamp stepped down into the street.

He did not look to left or right; he went away up the house-fronts at a stroll, toward the open place, with his hands behind him, like somebody who has dined.

"Count thirty," said Seln. "Then follow me, and not him."

The open place was empty. There were forty feet of trough, with the pipe running into its upper end, and not a beast at it, and the smith's two fires were banked. The man in the hood went to the pipe, and bent, and drank from his hand. He drank for a long time, and stood afterward with the water dripping from his fingers and looked at nothing, in the middle of a paved space with no cover on it for sixty yards.

Cael stood in a doorway and looked at a place twenty strides beyond him.

It was a great deal harder than not looking at a crown. The crown did not move.

A light came round the corner by the chandler's, low, jogging, with a rumble of small wheels. It was the watch: two men in leather caps, and a handcart between them, and a lantern slung from a pole at its front.

Seln's hand came up beside his coat, flat, palm down. *Stand.* Then two fingers.

They fell in behind the cart. It was done as a thing is done on a road, at a walk, without any word; and Cael understood it only when he was there. A watch looks where its own lantern falls; and anybody who turned to see that light would see the light, and two caps, and behind them a darkness in which a pair of porters might have been going home.

The watchmen did not turn round at all. One of them was telling the other about a goose.

They crossed the open place so, twenty strides behind a hood, to the mouth of the brokers' street. There the cart swung right, for the ring road; and the man with the cut boots went straight on.

It was as wide as a field, and there was nothing in it. Every slate had been wiped, and every bench stood empty against its wall.

"In an empty street nobody hides," said Seln, into his collar. "He goes home. You have the right-hand houses; I have the left. Sixty yards. Do not match his step."

Cael went down the right-hand side of the brokers' street, in the open, as a porter goes who has been kept late and has a bed at the bottom of the hill. His boots made a noise, and he let them. He looked at a place twenty strides ahead of a long coat, and the coat walked into it, and he chose another.

*What does it weigh?* asked the read, of a tread sixty yards in front of him that he could have had through the soles of his feet, heel and toe, for as long as he liked.

*No.*

*There?* asked the thin held note, of the lane's mouth half-way down, where the man was turning.

He told that one no as well, and he had begun to count them.

---

The lane went down toward the river, and it smelled of tar and new hemp.

Cael knew where it led before he saw. He had stood in that place with a big man, nine days ago, by daylight, and watched him look into the cut end of a rope as a person looks into a flower. It had been full of people walking backward then.

It was a shed, open all down one side, and it ran away along the inside of the town wall for three hundred yards. There was a great wheel at its head, and beyond the wheel a floor of trodden boards as straight as a ruled line, under a roof on posts, with coils hanging the whole length of the wall like hats on a row of pegs. A lantern burned at the far end, very small, where the tar yard was.

The man in the hood walked in under the roof, past the wheel, and went on down the middle of the boards.

Seln's fist had shut; then the palm came down flat, and stayed.

They stood at the corner of the last house, and Cael could hear the boots on the planking, unhurried, going away.

"It is a pipe," said Seln. He scarcely moved his lips. "Whoever goes into it behind him stands against that wheel and the sky for three hundred yards. Whoever goes round it by the open side has lost him for as long; and there is a quay at the bottom, and a lane up from the quay, and a wicket in the town wall for the tar-men. He has three doors out of the far end, and we should not know which."

"He knows that."

"He chose it because of that. I should have." The grey eyes went along the roof. "I shall stay at the wheel, in case he has only gone in to come out again. You will go round by the open side, behind the drying-racks, to the barrels. Go softly, and be there before he is. And when you are there, you are a barrel."

"And if he comes out past me?"

"Then let him. Count where he goes. I shall find you."

Cael went.

There was a strip of beaten ground along the shed's open side, and a line of racks on it, taller than a man, hung with yarn. He kept the racks between himself and the boards, and ran on the outer edges of his feet, as he had been taught on a different road by a different person, and he did not run fast.

*Away?*

Ten strides at a burst; thirty bursts; and a crack at the end of each like a carter's whip, in a town asleep. He told it no without breaking step.

He made one mistake, and it was not with his feet.

He glanced along the shed, to judge how much of it was left; he only meant to measure. But it was three hundred yards of a thing that somebody had built to go in one direction, with every post and plank and peg of it laid toward the far end; and the new thing behind his eyes came round to that as a weathercock comes round, before he had told it anything.

*Goes*, it said. The whole roof leaned away from him, down the length of the wall, toward a lantern.

He shut it, and it had been open for the space of a blink. A grain of sand came into each eye and sat there, and he ran the last hundred yards with his lids half down, and was angry, and had not the time to be.

The tar yard was a square of cobbles above the river, with barrels stood on end in rows and a black kettle on a cold hearth. He went in among the barrels and found two that stood a little apart, and put himself between them with his back to the wall.

Then he waited for a man to come out of the end of the shed.

He counted, since there was nothing else to do with the time. A person at a stroll goes three hundred yards in a count of two hundred and forty, or thereabouts. He gave it three hundred, and then four.

Nobody came.

The lantern burned on its post at the head of the quay steps, and under it the river went by with a noise like cloth being drawn over a table. He could see the shed's mouth, a square of blacker dark, twelve yards off; and nothing came out of it, and there was no sound of boots on boards.

At five hundred he understood.

The man had stopped. He was standing somewhere in that long roofed dark, among the coils, with his hands behind him, and he was doing what Cael was doing: he was listening for a second pair of feet to come down the boards after him, or round by the racks; and he was prepared to stand there until he heard them, or until it was morning.

Cael had been told what to do when a man stops. He had not been told what to do when the man stopped out of sight, and stayed, and there was no walking by.

He knew one thing that might serve, and he let his breath go long. He counted it, to ten and to ten again, as a healer had taught him on a plank; and he gave over wanting to be anywhere.

*This far?* said the eleventh, of the shed's mouth.

It was a good place for it, and he saw that plainly. A line stood from that post to the wall would have kept a man in a pipe like a cork in a bottle, until a grey porter came down from the wheel and they had him between them. It would have roared across half the district, and he told it no.

And Shadow asked.

It had not once left off, and it asked now as water asks at a sluice. He was standing between two tar barrels at night, in a black coat, making himself a part of the dark by holding his breath; and there was a thing under a seal that would have made him so in truth, to any eyes at all, between one heartbeat and the next.

*No*, he said. He had said it to that one twice a day since Ostrand. He said it now as a person leans on a door with somebody leaning on the other side.

At seven hundred and ten the boards spoke.

---

The boots came down the last of the shed without haste, and the man walked out of its mouth into the yard and stood still.

He was five yards off. Cael could have counted the buttons on the long coat, if there had been light to count by, and there was none on that side. The lantern was at the man's back, and it made of him a flat black shape with a hood on it and nothing under the hood at all.

The hood turned.

It went round the yard slowly, as a lamp is carried round a room: over the kettle, and the hearth, and the rows of barrels on end. It came to the two that stood apart, and rested there.

Cael did not breathe, and he did not want to be elsewhere. He had learned that in the corners of halls, and at the edges of yards, and it had never been asked of him by anybody who stood so near.

He counted ten. The hood went on round, as slowly as it had come, to the wall and the quay steps; and the man went down the steps.

At the foot, for the length of two strides, the lantern was on him from above. Cael saw a pair of horseman's boots go through its light, with a square of paler leather at each heel. He saw gloved hands, clasped behind, and the back of a hood.

He did not know whether he had been seen. He thought, afterward, that the man had looked at the place where a follower would be standing if there were one, and had neither found him nor needed to.

"You did not come through after him," said a voice at his shoulder. "That was well judged."

Cael had not heard a step, and he let his breath go.

"He stood in there. I counted seven hundred."

"He stood for eleven minutes. I heard him not walking, which is a sound that I know." Seln was looking down at the quay. "He is in no hurry at all. I begin to dislike that more than I should dislike a man who ran."

The quay was long and bare; six flat boats lay along it, nose to tail, with their sweeps shipped. Beyond them the great wheel of the hoist stood up against the water, and beyond that again a cable went out over the river into the dark.

Cael looked at the cable, and his stomach went cold.

"There's a ferry."

"There is. It has no crew before first light. But a man who had one waiting would be on the other bank in a quarter of an hour, and we should be standing here."

The man in the hood walked the whole length of that quay, and went by the six boats, and laid a hand on the mooring post of each as he passed, as a person touches the heads of children. He stopped at the cable, and looked along it at the far shore; and he stood so long that Cael began again to count.

Then he turned his back on the river, and went up a flight of steps between two warehouses, toward the hill.

"He has shown us the ferry," said Seln. "He wished us to think of it."

They followed by the steps. There was a lane above, and at its head the ring road, lying under the second wall like a ditch under a dyke; and there the man turned right, and went along it at his stroll, with the pale stone going up on his left hand higher than a house.

Cael knew that road. He had walked the whole round of it on his second day in the town, by himself, and had not once lifted his eyes above the top of that wall. He did not lift them now; he looked at a place twenty strides ahead of a long coat, on the cobbles, and went home.

It was a quarter of the way round the bluff, and it took the better part of an hour, and the man did not once look round. He stopped twice: once to do up a boot, with his back to them, for a long minute; and once for no reason, in the middle of the way, with his hands behind him. Each time one of the two walked on past him, on the far side, with his chin down; and each time the other had him.

The watch's cart came toward them, by the chandler's, with its lantern jogging. The man in the hood lifted two fingers to it as it passed, and one of the leather caps nodded.

"They know him," said Cael, in the dark of a warehouse door.

"They know that he may be out. It is a different thing, and it is the thing that matters." Seln's voice was as mild as ever. "You and I have gone round this town tonight like mice in a wall. He has gone round it like a man taking the air."

The road brought them out, at last, by the back of the smith's, into the open place where the trough was. They had come the whole way round to within eighty yards of their own beds.

The man crossed the open place, and went by the smith's two cold fires, and set his foot on the one street that climbed.

"No nearer," said Seln, and drew Cael into the smith's doorway. "Look where we are standing."

It was a deep doorway, well back from the lamp at the corner. A man had stood in it five nights ago, at an hour when nobody has business in a doorway, and looked at the carriage-arch of an inn.

At the head of the street, two hundred yards above them, a brazier burned red in the arch of the second wall. The grating was down; it filled the arch from the keystone to the paving, a net of black iron with the fire behind it, and in front of the fire stood two shapes in long coats.

The man in the hood walked up the middle of the street to them.

He did not show anything at once; he went to the brazier, and held out his gloved hands to it, and stood so, with his back to the whole district, for as long as it takes to say a rhyme. One of the keepers spoke. The man turned his head, and answered, and put a hand into his coat; whatever came out of it was held low, in the palm, and was looked at, and put away.

A small door opened in the foot of the grating.

He stooped to it. And in it, with one boot through, he turned round.

The brazier was beside him, and its light came up under the hood. There was something pale there, two hundred yards off, no larger than a thumbnail held at arm's length, and it was turned down the street toward a smith's doorway. It stayed for the space of one breath.

Then he went in, and the small door shut.

---

"I'm going up," said Cael.

"To do what?"

"To see where it stops."

Seln looked at him, and did not say that it was foolish. He said, "A person may walk up a street. Do not speak first, and do not stand as though you were waiting to be let in."

So Cael walked up it, in the middle, as the other had.

It was two hundred yards, and he had gone up them on eight mornings with a card in front of him and a box on his back; he had never done it with his hands empty. The brazier grew as he climbed, and the two long coats took on a colour, which was dark blue.

*This far?*

It had never asked so loud. Every other place where it had asked had been a doorway that he chose to see as a line. This was a line. Somebody had cut a groove across a road, a hand wide, and lined it with iron, and hung a net of bars above it to come down at dusk; and it had come down. The thing in him knew its own kind. It asked in the voice of a bar going home into two sockets, and it very nearly did not need an answer.

He stopped a hand short of the iron.

The keepers had watched him come. One of them had a pike, grounded, and the other a board on a cord round his neck, and they were not unfriendly. The one with the board looked him over, from the cap to the boots, and saw what there was to see.

"First bell," he said, quite kindly. "And you'll want your card. There's no kerb-work to be had up here before the light."

Cael nodded. He did not speak, since he had been told not to, and since there was nothing that he could have said to that man which would not have been a lie or a great deal too much.

He stood at the groove for as long as a porter might, who has come up early by mistake.

He could have crossed it; he looked at that squarely, with the fire on his face. There was a thing at his knees that would have had him through the small door on anybody's heels, and neither of those two would have known that the air had moved. There was a hiss in his feet which made nothing of walls. He had twelve things in him, and not a keeper in that arch could have named one.

He could not cross it lawfully, and that was another matter, and it was the matter that the groove was for.

By day he might go through as the fourth part of a figure on another person's card, for a named door and a stated purpose, and be counted out again by dusk; by right he might not go through at all. Iron walked under that arch, and showed a paper, and was written. There was a colour for Iron, and a paler one for the wall above. A plate had put a word after his name that had no colour. There was no card in the district with an edge for what he was; and a man with his spurs cut off had just put a hand into his coat and brought out the one thing that Cael would never own, which was leave.

He had been stopped by lines for as long as he could remember being anything. They had all been written ones, in files, in rooms that he was not let into. He had never before been able to stand at one, and see the iron, and measure with his eye how wide it was.

It was a hand wide, and he found that he was glad to know it.

*No*, he told the eleventh; and he turned his back on a brazier, and went down the street.

---

Seln had not stirred from the doorway.

"Well?"

"He's Iron, or he's got a paper that does as well. He showed it low. They opened the little door." Cael leaned on the smith's jamb. His legs had begun to tell him how far they had been. "That's all. We walked three hours to learn that a man can go through a gate."

"Eleven guards on one broker's doors can go through that gate at night, and every Iron in the district. I do not call it a finding. I call it a wall." Seln was looking up the street at the red point of the fire. "But I shall tell you what I have, since you have earned the half of it. He was not learning whether he was followed. He knew that from the trough. He took us round by the one shed where we must divide, and stood eleven minutes in it; he showed us a ferry; he showed us a watch that nods to him; and then he went to bed, through a thing that we cannot pass, and turned in the door so that we should be sure of having seen."

"Why?"

"So that we should know how much time they have. That is the whole of it. They need not take you in a stair. They may stand under a lamp for a month."

Cael thought about that, with the cold of the doorway at his back.

"He wanted me to do something," he said. "At the barrels. Or at that gate. He stood five yards off and looked at where I was, for a count of ten. It'd have been the easiest thing I've ever done, to be gone."

"Yes."

"I don't think it was the stair they shut, two nights ago. I think it was this. They want it shown, and they'll wait."

"Then they have been shown a porter, twice." The small grey man came out of the doorway. "It was worth one night to learn that it is not worth two. I shall not follow him again; and I should be obliged if you did not."

They went back by the muck-lane. The fist came out from under two blankets as they passed the end stall, and opened, palm upward, on the straw. *Nothing?*

Cael turned his own hand over, empty, and the old man shut his eyes.

There was a greyness over the roofs by then, and he washed at the pump, and the tar would not come off his palms; he had not known that he had leaned upon a barrel. He wrote three lines by the last of the lantern, with the book held at the length of his arm.

*Night of the 291st. Followed the boots, with S., round the whole of the lower town. All of them asked; Shadow asked for three hours. One look that I didn't mean, with the twelfth, at the rope-walk: a grain in each eye. Not counted. Nothing opened.*

*He may go through the blue gate at night. I may not go through it at all, except as a figure. It is a hand wide.*

*S.: they have time. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*

The second bell was three hours off. He lay down in his coat, and slept like a thing dropped.

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md

# Chapter 37 — How Far

The bridge was a made thing that morning, and it had never been one before.

It was the two hundred and ninety-second morning past the Line, and Cael saw it from the kerb of the carts' street, where the hill dropped away and all the roofs of the district lay below. It stood out on the brown water as it had stood the day before, small and dark, on five piers. He had looked at it then as a person looks at a road that he came in by. Now it was the oldest thing that he could see from that hill which anybody had built. He had that on the word of a small pale woman, who had laid her hand where the masons' pale stone was cut to fit it. And the new thing behind his eyes came round to it, across half a mile of air, like a head lifted from a book.

He did not know whether it could read so far. He found that he did not wish to learn it on a street, with three hours' sleep in him and a lamp to hold.

He put his eyes on the tail-board of the handcart. That was no better. The tail-board had a hinge, and the hinge leaned.

It was the same wherever he turned. A river is not made, and a man is not; but a town is nothing else, from the ditch to the weathercock, and he had been given a thing that inclined toward every peg and course of it as a dog's nose goes to every post.

He had gone through this once already, with another matter. For eleven days he had not looked at a light on a crown, and he had not done it by shutting his eyes. He had done it by having somewhere else to put them, and a reason, twice a day, until his neck no longer set at an alley. He did the same now. He looked at a raw hand on a tarred case, which nobody had made, and at the back of a girl's neck, and pushed.

"You're walking like a horse in blinkers," said Lira.

"I'm learning not to look at things."

"You'd that already. You've had it since the rise."

"This is a different not-looking. It's the same road."

She thought about that for the length of ten strides.

"You're going to run out of places to put your eyes," she said.

The clerk wrote *the same*, and *four*. The old man went down twenty-seven steps on his seat, with his heel in Cael's palm, and said at the foot that he believed he had improved upon his time.

There was tar on the lamp's ring before the first sheet had been looked at.

Cael saw it when he changed his grip: a black thumb's breadth on the brass, off a palm that a pump had not been able to clean. The man in the dusty coat saw it a moment after. He had stopped, with the reed lifted.

"There's tar on my lamp."

He said it to the ring. His red eyes had come to the brass, and to the smear upon it, and they stayed there as though a line had been drawn at the edge of the metal; they did not go on by the width of a finger to the hand that held it. For seven days they had gone over that hand as over a hook. This was not the same. Cael watched a man take care, and he knew what the care was for.

"It'll come off with a rag," said the rough voice. "After. Left."

The flame was there.

It was a long day, and Karis made it longer by being right four times.

The man gave her his joins from the second bell. He went along the Ring's eleven with his finger, and the Stair's twenty-nine, and at every place where a run came down flat he said *red*, and she laid a square of tile. By noon each of the fifty-two sheets was parted, in broken tile, into pieces of the length of words. She had the tall books open along the table's edge. She laid a word against a word.

"Eighteen," she said, in the forenoon; and at noon, with her bread untouched, "Nineteen."

The first thing that she read which was not a number came a little after.

Cael saw her find it. It was a group of three signs, and it stood so often on the Stair's long table that she had passed it over as packing; and she went back to it, and looked from the pencil to the brown book, and sat quite still.

"It is *and*," she said. "It is only *and*. It is the commonest word that the old clerk has, and it is the commonest on these floors; there are nine of them on this one sheet." She laughed, which she seldom did, and put the back of her bound hand to her mouth. "I have waited a year to read a word of this, and the first word is one that a child has before it can walk."

"That's a good word," said Brom. "Nothing's built without it."

She had twenty by the time the flame was trimmed, and it was then that she took the glove off.

There was a group of six signs on the Stair's long table which stood four times in those twenty-nine, and always after a white. She had not let herself look at it for four days; she had laid a glove across the place whenever her work took her near. She lifted the glove now, by one finger. She had every letter of the six, and she did not read them at once. She looked first at the man with the reed.

"I had a word that I wished to find here," she said. "I had it when I knew four of these, and I covered the place so that I should not see it. I am going to say now that I wished for a name. I thought that a man who cut his orders into three floors might have signed them."

"And?"

"It is not a name." Her eyes had gone along the six. "It is *set*. As a stone is set, or a stake: set this, and what, and how many. It is a better word than mine. It is the one that is there."

"Twenty-one," she said, at the bell, and her voice was nearly gone.

The man had written his card. He stood by the desk, and his hand went up to the shelf, to the thick bundle tied both ways with tape gone brown; and two fingers rested there, as they had rested on the day when he named what it was made of.

"Not tonight. I'll not have that tape cut by anybody who's listening for a bell." He took his fingers away. "In the morning. It's the seventh. Come down with your eyes rested, if you know how that's done."

---

There was a mule in the inn yard when they brought the handcart in under the arch, and a man with his hat cut away on one side.

Cael knew the hat before he knew the man. He had seen it across a river with ice in it, on the far bank, on the day that the last ferry ran; and there had been a bag under that arm then, as there was now. It was the carrier who took whatever went between the towns of the Line and one plank under an eave, some days east.

The man was looking at Brom. More exactly, he was looking at Brom's back, between the shafts.

"I've seen that go up a bank," he said. "A fortnight since, at the western landing, with a box on it; and a tall girl, and a little one with a roll of hide. You're off the one-armed man's cloth. The eleventh."

"We were," said Brom. "We're here now."

"So I see. I'd have walked past you." The carrier had put his hand into the bag, without looking, as a man does who knows what lies where. "I lie here tonight, and I'm for the landing at first light. There's a thing in this for his plank. He'll say it out twice, the way he does, and there'll be no boy standing there to take it; and it'll come back to me by the next boat with a cross on it." He brought it out. "*For the boy with the book.* I'm not to break a bag for a stranger. I'll break it for a thumb that the old man knows."

It was small, and thick, and wrapped in oilcloth. There was a knot on it that Cael could have undone in the dark.

Brom set his thumb to the carrier's way-slip, in the grease of the cart's own hub, beside the line that named the parcel; it was a mark that a one-armed man had seen on a good many slips, and would know. Cael found a copper of his own for the carrying. No name was asked for, and none was put down.

He pulled the one end of cord that would loose that knot, sitting on the mounting block, with the others round him.


exec
/bin/zsh -lc "sed -n '1201,1600p' editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-006/review-editorial-prompt.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
They were of brass. There were two legs, each as long as his hand, flat on the inner side so that they lay close. At the foot of each was a point of blued steel, as fine as a thorn, the pair of them thrust into a round of cork. The legs met at the head in a joint with a broad flat rivet, and above the rivet stood a little turned stalk for the fingers. They had been rubbed with something, so that the last of the light ran down them like water.

A leaf from an order-pad was folded round the legs. It was in a square upright hand.

*A rule says a line is true. A divider says how far, and carries it. You've the first. Here's the second, and mind the points; they'll go through a thumb as easy as a sheet. Lines are for finding out how far a thing is from another thing. Everything is. — H.*

He read it twice over, and the yard was very quiet.

An old man in Denvash had cut that leaf from his pad, and had rolled a pair of dividers in it, and tied his knot. He had sent a ruler before the snow, to a boy who was going where there were no instruments. He could not have known that there was a hide with four rings on it, or a line through the rings which ran off the edge of the skin, or that somebody would shortly need to know how far along that line the next thing lay. He had only thought, at his bench, that a person who has a straight edge will want a pair of legs to step it off with.

"He's sent you a thing to stab yourself on," said Lira. "He does know you."

"He says to mind the points."

"He said to mind the shape, with the last one. He's working his way down to your fingers."

Cael drew the cork off, and opened them.

He ought not to have looked at the joint. He had been not looking at things since the second bell; but this lay in his own two hands, and it was a gift, and for the space of a breath he was only a boy with a new tool.

*Bears*, said the twelfth, of the rivet.

It had come round before he could stop it. Both legs hung from that one pin; he saw the whole of the thing lean inward to it, the two brass arms and the steel at their feet, as everything on a gate leans to its hinge. It was true, and it was no more than his eyes could have told him. The sand came into them all the same.

He shut the legs, and put the cork back upon the points.

"What?" said Lira.

"It looked. I didn't tell it to."

"At a pair of compasses?"

"It'd look at a spoon."

Karis had put out her right hand. She did not take them; she held the hand a little way off, as she had once held it above a page.

"I should like the loan of those," she said. "Not now. When I have a hide in front of me again, and a point upon it to carry from. I have been stepping that line off with a strip of brass and my thumbnail." Her eyes had not left the points. "I am going up. I have twenty-one, and I have found the twenty-first only once."

"There's a dinner," said Brom.

"Then keep me what will keep."

---

It was Brom's ruling, and he made it at the half-door with the purse in his fist.

"A parcel's come the breadth of the country to a hayloft, and found it. That's eaten on. We've had the pot for ten nights and said nothing against it. Tonight we'll have what she'd give a carter with money."

"Four," said the woman, when he had told her what he wanted. She looked at him as she had looked on the first day, when he asked for a seventh bowl. "In front."

He counted four marks onto the ledge, and she swept them off it with the side of her hand.

"A hundred and thirty," he said, to the purse.

They ate in the lower room, off the yard, since it was level with the cobbles and one of them could be brought there on a shoulder. It had a single long table, and a hatch, and a candle in a dish. What came through the hatch was a goose. There was a pie after it with a lid as broad as a shield, and a white loaf, and a dish of apples baked in something sweet; and there was a jug of cider, cold, from which Brom poured seven cups.

"There's five of you sitting," said the woman, at the hatch.

"There's seven cups."

She had learned by now that it was his pot. She put two saucers on the board, and went away.

He set a saucer over each of the two cups, to keep the dust out, and stood them at the table's end. One was a small pale woman's, who was above their heads with a lantern and twenty-one letters. The other had been carried under the lashing of his box, as an empty bowl, for eleven days; and the person whom it was kept for was a long way north along a hill-foot, with a mule.

Cael ate until he could not. He had not known how thin a thing a pot of barley was until there was a goose beside it.

He made his second mistake of the day over the pie.

He had his elbows on the board, and his eyes had gone down to the wood, idly, as a person's do who is full. It was an old table. It had been mended. *Bears*, the thing said, of the two trestles in the middle; and of the leg at the far end, by the old man's stick, it said *nothing*, as flatly as it had said so of a red square of tile.

He looked away, at the candle, and that was worse. He looked at his own hands.

"That leg's doing no work," said Brom.

He had spoken with his mouth full. He leaned back, and knocked the far leg with the side of his boot, twice, and it gave a little dry rattle in its socket.

"It's been let in since. For the look of it, I'd say, after somebody sat on the end and it tipped. The weight's all on the two middle ones; you can see where the top's bowed to them." He took up his cup. "I'd have pegged it through. It'd carry, then."

Cael began to laugh. He could not have stopped it. He put the heels of his hands to his eyes, which were running, and laughed into them.

"What have I said?"

"Nothing. You've said what I got told, a breath before, and it cost me. You did it with your boot."

"You go and look at what's sitting on it," said Brom, mildly. "I told that fellow so. He's a good eye, for a man who never lifted anything."

The dividers went round the table once, shut, with the cork on. Seln turned them over in his fingers and gave them back without a word, which was praise. The old man held them for some time. He opened the legs a little way, and set the two points upon the board, an inch apart, and looked at the inch.

"It carries a distance," he said. "It does not say what the distance means. I have signed a great many instruments in my time that did the second and could not do the first."

He shut them, and passed them on.

It was near the end, when the apples had gone, that Seln put two fingers into his sleeve.

He drew out a slip of paper, folded once. Cael had not seen it go in. It was no bigger than a card, and there was writing on it, close, on one face. The small grey man read it through by the candle, with no more change in his face than when he read a broker's slate. Then he held one corner of it in the flame.

It took, and he turned it as it burned, and laid the last of it in the dish, and watched until it was black.

"Closed."

Nobody asked him what; he had not offered it to be asked. Cael counted the word, as he counted anything, and put it beside three others that the same man had given him at a pump; and he understood this one no better, and found that he had not expected to.

Vastin had been looking at the two covered cups.

He had looked at them a good deal, through the goose and the pie, as he looked at an entry that he could not at once place. He had sat at more boards than anybody in that room. At every one of them a cover had been laid for whoever was present, in the order of the roll, and a person who was not in the room had no place in it.

"One of those is for a lady who is in a hayloft with a pencil," he said. "The other is for a woman who went north eleven days ago, and does not know that there is a table."

"That's so," said Brom.

[[TIER-B: "Out here you count who's owed a seat," … "It's a different ledger." — the plan quotes this unit with an elision (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier C, S14) and does not pin its speaker (the packet has only "Vastin and the cups"); staged at the long table of the inn's lower room, after Vastin has remarked on the two cups kept under saucers for Karis and Oryn and Brom has said "That's so". The lines before and after are written so that either Vastin (as a thing he has worked out) or Brom (as his answer) could own it; coordinator to supply protected text]]

Nobody added to that. The old man turned his own cup a quarter round upon the board, and looked into it; and Brom reached over with the jug, since he could reach, and filled it.

---

Karis had her twenty-first twice when they came up.

She said so from the boxes, without lifting her head, in what was left of her voice. Brom set the covered cup down by her knee. She drank it cold, in three swallows, with her eyes on a leaf, and Cael did not think that she had tasted it.

He sat down against the third box with Hesk's book.

He had not meant to make the inventory that night. He made one at the start of a thing; and he could not have said, climbing the ladder, what was starting. But he had a twelfth, which was not yet in any count. He had a pair of brass legs in his coat from a man who had taught him to write the count first. And there was a bundle on a shelf, two hundred yards over his head, which a pair of square pale fingers would untie in the morning.

He had kept his day's lines in that town, as he kept them anywhere. This was the other thing, the long one; and it was the first of its kind that he had ever set down inside a wall.

He wrote the head.

*Treswick; the loft over the stable; one in the stall. The two hundred and ninety-second day. Eight days inside. A hundred and thirty: four to a dinner, on Brom's ruling, and worth it. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*

He ruled a line under that, and wrote the second entry slowly, and left nothing out.

*Nineteen. Twelve confirmed fragments.*

*Wind — Lira. Pressure — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron — Brom. Compression — Reydan. Ember — Karis. Shadow — Seln; source protected; sealed; twice in all; it asked for three hours last night and was told no. Storm — Daeva. Anchor — unnamed. Blade — Ephram. Tide — Oryn; ask first, every time; used on nobody this side of the snow, and five times on myself, the last two round the place and not in. Iron Wall — Vastin; held; entered, not told; deployment none. Rune — the reader; a made thing's lean; given; told; deployment none; it looks before I've said that it may, and I am learning to give it nothing to look at.*

*One anomaly, as before. The still place. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Not touched since the waypost, and not to be until a healer has been asked.*

*Held, with no ruling: a cord, two edges, a hair. A row that counted a sheet short. I have not laid a hand to an instrument since the hundred and sixty-eighth day.*

That was two. He took the dividers out of his coat and laid them, shut, on the open leaf, and wrote the third round them.

*Hesk sent dividers. Lines are for finding out how far a thing is from another thing. Everything is. I'll enter that as a rule and see what it's worth.*

He looked at the brass for a moment. He had a ruling upon himself, a hundred and twenty-four days old, that he was not to try a measure with his own hand; and here was a thing whose whole purpose was to be opened upon a distance and carried. He did not open it. He had a hair in a small pale woman's notebook to remind him why.

---

The line moved while the brass still lay upon the leaf.

There was a blanket on the gable window, hung on two nails by a girl on their first night, and it had hung there since. It did not quite meet the frame on the left side. Each night a thread of the crown's light came in by that gap, and lay across the boards a yard from his knee, pale and thin and as straight as a chalk line. He had never looked at it as a light. It was a mark on a floor, as the bars in the lane were marks on the cobbles, and he stepped over it going to his pallet.

It lay tonight where it always lay. He had the tail of his eye on it, and no more.

For a count of one, it was not steady.

It did not go out; it thickened, and thinned, as a lamp's flame does when a hand is put near the wick; and it was as it had been. He had counted it before he knew that he was counting. *One.*

He sat quite still, with the pencil off the leaf.

There was a weight on him. He knew it, and he would have given a good deal not to. He had stood in a chalk ring once, in a room with no window, and had felt a thing at the edge of his sight attend to him for eleven seconds while a plate stayed dark. This was fainter than that, and it came from farther off. But it was of the same kind. It was the particular weight of an instrument turning toward him.

He made himself go on from there, since that was the place where a person stops thinking and begins to be afraid.

A needle swings to iron. Nobody says that the needle has seen the iron. A cord had read a stride short in his hand, and nobody had said that the cord knew him; a strip of brass had gone a hair past its mark, and it was brass. He had been a thing that instruments went wrong on since a morning in a frozen yard, and for a good while before it. A draught will move a blanket. A lamp-trimmer will put his hand to a lamp. And his own eyes had been paying for another matter since the afternoon.

It was three strides to that window.

He looked at the three strides. The old man slept in a stall now. The pallet by the gable had been rolled, and there was nothing between his knee and the blanket but boards. He could cross them. He could lift one corner of a horse-blanket, and see for himself whether the thing on the crown burned as it had always burned.

He had written a sentence at a fork-stone, in this book, at a square woman's asking, and had read it back to her. It did not say *the steps* and *the square* because those were the only two ways of going near. It said them because she had seen what he did with a question.

He did not cross.

He did not look again at the line, either; he turned upon the boards, so that the third box was between his shoulder and it, and drew the lantern nearer. He wrote the fourth entry on that page in the form that he had used once before, on the hundred and sixty-eighth day, for a thing that he could neither prove nor put down.

*Claim: the light on the crown was not steady, for a count of one. Evidence: a pale line on the boards of a loft, through the edge of a blanket; one witness, with tired eyes. No cause found. Ruling: hold.*

Under it he put the last, since it was owed in that book and to nobody else.

*The promise stands. Not the steps. Not the square.*

He shut the dividers into the page, and the book on them.

Lira was awake. She was lying on her right side, with the slow arm uppermost, and she had watched him turn his back to the gable.

"What was it?"

"It's in there. Fourth down."

She read it by the lantern, with her lips going. She looked once at the blanket, and at the gap beside it, and then she got up without a word, in her shirt, and crossed the three strides that he had not. She did not lift the corner. She drew the blanket a hand's breadth along its nails, until it met the frame, and pressed the edge flat with her thumb.

There was no line on the boards.

"Twenty-seven days out of the wool," she said, of the arm that had done it. "It's good for something."

---

The fourth of the five gates had a child in its fourteenth year, and Oryn was asked to stand by.

She came to it at dusk on that same day, two days behind her own reckoning and no longer troubling to count them. It was a larger hold than the others. Its wall was of stone, as high as a man's head, with a fence of split oak above; and the bench under its gate-house eave was long enough for twelve.

There were nine on it, and she went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist. A herdsman's foot that a beast had trodden. A baby with a rash, and a mother who had not slept. An old woman who wished only to be told that the thing in her chest was the same thing as last year, and it was. She asked each one, and had each one's yes, and it was full dark before she had her hands in the butt.

The old woman had waited. She was the one who kept that hold's days, as some woman did in each of them: when the rams went in, and when the beans; who was owed what, and whose turn it was at the gate.

"There's a thing at first light," she said. "I'd take it kindly if you were on that bench for it. Not to do anything. Only to be there."

"What thing?"

"My daughter's girl is in her year. Fourteen since the hay. We ring them on the threshing floor, at the first light after the moon's full, and it was full last night." She had her arms folded in her apron. "It's never gone amiss here. But her mother's mother went down like a felled calf, at hers, and lay till noon. It's in the family to take it hard."

"I'll not lay a hand on her unless she's hurt, and unless she says. She'll have enough that's new without a stranger's fingers."

"I never asked for your hand. I asked for the bench."

"Then you've the bench," said Oryn.

She ate what was brought out, and lay down beside the mule.

She had seen it done forty times on that road, at one gate or another, and had never got used to how little there was to see. Inside the Line it was a matter of offices. She had stood in one herself: a room with a desk, and a ring chalked on the boards, and a clerk who looked at a plate of brass and not at her. The district had named her day a year beforehand. Out here there was no desk, and no plate, and nobody to write. An old woman scratched a ring on a threshing floor with a hazel stick, on a morning that she chose by the moon; and the child stood in it; and it came.

Whether it needed the ring, Oryn had never learned. Nobody at any of those gates had been willing to try it without.

---

The floor was of beaten clay, in the open, with a low kerb of stones about it.

They were all there by the first grey: the whole hold, thirty or more, standing off beyond the kerb with their breath smoking, and not one of them speaking above a murmur. Oryn sat on the bench by the gate. It was forty yards. She had her hands in her sleeves.

The old woman drew the ring.

She walked it backward, bent, with the stick's point in the clay; and when the two ends met she stepped over the line, and held her hand out, and a girl came across the floor to her.

She was thin, and tall for her age, with her hair in one plait and a sheepskin on her shoulders that was too big. She was frightened, and she was trying to walk as though she were not. Oryn had a square face that showed little, and she kept it so; but she had looked for that walk, and it was there, as it always was. Every one of them came across the clay like a person carrying a full pail.

The girl stepped into the ring, and the old woman stepped out of it.

Nothing happened that a watcher could have named.

There was no light, and no sound, unless it was a ewe somewhere behind the wall. A thin child stood in a scratched circle with her hands shut at her sides, and a hold stood round her and held its breath.

Then her head turned.

It was a small movement, a little to the left, as a person turns toward a voice. Her eyes went with it. They went to a place in the air that was just beyond the corner of her sight, and when they came there the place had moved, and they followed it. Oryn watched a girl of fourteen look at something a hand's breadth from her own cheek that not one of thirty people could see.

She had seen that forty times as well. It was always the eyes. A child found, on such a morning, that there was a thing in the world which belonged to nobody else, and which nobody else would ever be shown; and the first that she did was to try to look straight at it, and fail, and try again.

The girl's lips had parted. She was listening now. Whatever it was had said its one word, down at the bottom of the breastbone, where nobody could overhear.

Her face came open like a door.

The old woman was over the line before the child's knees had begun to go. They did go, a little. She was caught, and held, with a grey head bent to hers. Oryn, on a bench forty yards off, saw the girl's mouth move against her grandmother's ear, three or four words, quick, and saw the old arms tighten.

That was the whole of it, and the hold let its breath go. Somebody laughed, and somebody was crying, and a small boy broke away from his mother's skirt and ran across the kerb to see what his sister had got.

She would not be told what the word had been, and she did not wish to be; it was theirs.

Oryn sat with her hands in her sleeves, and found that she was not thinking of the girl.

She was thinking of a boy on a table, under her two hands, a long while since, who had told her a thing with his breath going slow. He had stood in a ring too, and it had been chalk, and there had been a clerk. A small light had come up from behind his breastbone to the edge of his eye, as it had come just now to that child; and it had looked at him, for a count that he still knew; and then it had gone out. She had asked him whether it hurt. He had said no; he had said that it was like a wick being pinched.

This girl had looked up, when it was over, and found a face. Every one of the forty had. It was the second thing that they did.

She did not know whose face he had found. There had been a clerk in that room, and a plate of brass; she did not suppose that either had been looking at him.

She had put her hands on that boy more than once. She had found in him what she had found in nobody, and had told him to write it down and leave it be; and then she had walked north from a fork, owing him the one reading that she had not given.

She was two days behind, and there was a fifth gate, and a ford, and a mill.

She made her reckoning there, on the bench, as she would have made it over a wrist. The fifth gate should have its day, since it had waited a cold term. The ford could have an hour. What she would not do was come late to that mill; six people would be there on the day that she had named, and one of them had a thing for her that she had told him to bring. If the road cost her a night's walking, then it cost that. And when she had him on the plank, she would not go round him again as she had gone round before. She would go as deep as she went. She would ask him first, and have his yes; and then she would read what was there, and say what she found, whether she had a word for it or not.

The old woman had come across the clay. She stood by the bench with her arms folded, and her eyes were wet, and she was not troubling to hide it.

"You never stirred," she said.

"She never needed me. That's the best morning I've had on this road since the thaw."

"She kept her feet. Near enough."

"She did. Tell her that somebody saw."

Oryn got up, and went to see to the mule.

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md

# Chapter 38 — Read Once

The tall books went back first.

The man in the dusty coat had ruled it from his chair, on the two hundred and ninety-third morning, before any of them had a load off. It was the seventh day of his week; there were twelve of the old town's tally-books lying open along a table's edge, and he would not have a tape touched while one of them was out of its bay.

"As they stood. Third bay on the left, and the shelves she had them off. The lamp can go with them; I'll not want it here." His eyes were on the floor between two boxes. "I've a light of my own for this."

So Lira went up the ladder in the eastern gallery, with a book as broad as a paving stone against her chest, and Brom stood at the foot with the ring in his left fist.

"You said that I was a wall," he said, over his shoulder, to nobody who was listening.

"A wall will do for a ladder," said Lira, from the dark. "Higher. No. Lower."

Cael had turned to go with them. He had not been four strides from that flame since the third hour of the second day, and his hand felt wrong without it.

"Stay," said Karis.

She stood by the second chair, with her notebook against her chest. She had slept; he could see that she had, and that it had been done on purpose, as a person eats before a long road; and it had not taken the white out of her face.

"I should like one of you here. I do not wish to read it to a chair, and a man who shuts his eyes." She drew a breath. "You have knelt at this for eight days, and been given a sore knee for it. I think that you are owed the end of the week."

"I've nothing to hold."

"Then hold nothing. Stand there."

He stood at the desk's left side, a hand off, where the lamp would have been.

There was a fourth in that end of the gallery, and nobody had placed him. The old man sat on Brom's box by the nearest bay, six strides from the desk, with the bound leg out along its plank and both hands on the ash. He had come down the stair on his seat, and could not have followed a ladder to the eastern gallery, and he had not been asked to; he was only where he had been set down, as he was every morning.

The man reached up to the shelf.

He took the bundle in both hands; it was thicker than a loaf, and tied across both ways, and it left a clean square in the dust where it had lain. He set it upon the slope of his desk, under the good lamp, and stood looking at the knot.

"I'll not cut it," he said. "Whoever tied that meant it to be untied."

It took him some time. The tape had gone brown and stiff, and it cracked under his nails like the skin of an onion; but the knot came, a turn at a time, and he laid the two lengths out to either side as flat as rulers. Under the tape there were two thin boards of beech, and he lifted the upper one away.

Cael looked at what lay beneath, since he had been told to stand there, and since it was the thing that all of them had walked to.

It was a single leaf, and it was not paper.

It had the colour of old horn, and it was stiff, like horn, and a little curled at one corner; and where the lamp struck the curl, the light came through it, brown and clouded, as it comes through the pane of a stable lantern. Somebody had made it of a skin, a long while ago, and scraped it thin.

It had not been written with a pen; he saw that at once, from his side of the desk, with the head of it away from him. There was no ink that lay upon the surface. Every sign had been pressed into the skin with a point, as a thing is scored on a slate, and some dark stuff had been rubbed into the scoring afterward and had mostly gone. The lines stood in it like the tracks of a very small bird across a crust of snow.

They were the floors' letters, and he could not read one of them; but he had held a lamp above that woman's column for eight days, and he knew a hooked upright when he saw it, and a gate, and a fork.

There was a place in it that was not like the rest.

It lay a little below the middle, where for the width of four or five letters the skin was paler, and thinner, and rough, as a board is rough where a plane has gone against the grain. Something had been taken out of that line with an edge. Whoever did it had been thorough; and had stopped, all the same, a hair too soon. At the left side of the pale place a single stroke was left standing. It was upright, and as long as a letter is tall, and it led to nothing.

The thing behind Cael's eyes had come round to that leaf as it had come round to nothing since a plank of oak, and it leaned as a dog leans on a leash.

*No*, he told it. This was hers.

The man had bent over the skin. He looked at it from its foot, without a blink, and the water ran; and Cael, who had no lamp to move, found his empty hand going left of its own accord.

"One hand," said the rough voice at last. "One sitting. He ruled it first, with the same point; you can see the lines under the letters. It's a fair copy, and he didn't hurry." The square finger came to rest in the air above the pale place. "That's later. I can tell you that it's been scraped since it was written. I can't tell you by whom."

He straightened, and felt for the edge of the desk, and stood back from it by two paces with his eyes shut.

"I've looked," he said. "It's yours."

---

She did not sit.

She stood at the slope of the desk, where he had stood, with her notebook open beside the skin and her pencil upright in her right hand. The left lay flat upon the oak, in its strip of linen. Cael saw her eyes go to the first sign, at the head, in the corner that was farthest from him; and they stopped there.

They stopped for a long time.

He had watched that woman read since a reading room above Ostrand. She read as some people eat, quickly, with her whole attention, and a page was gone before another person had found its first line. She did not read so now; her eyes went along one sign at a time, and her lips were shut. Twice the pencil went to the notebook and wrote a single letter, and went back; and once she turned to the last of the tall books, which she had kept by her, and found something in it, and nodded, and shut it.

"There were two that I had not," she said, to nobody. "It spells them. It spells each of them twice."

Then she began again at the head.

Cael counted, since there was nothing else for him to do. He counted the lines on the skin, by the pale place, and made them twenty-six; he counted the breaths of the man beside him, which were slow. He looked at the lamp on its iron arm, and saw the oil in the glass stand a finger lower than when the tape came off.

Nobody spoke. From the eastern gallery, a long way off and round a corner of rock, there came now and then the small dry knock of a book going home upon a shelf.

She went through it three times. He knew when each ended, since her eyes came up from the foot and rested on the wall for the space of a breath. After the second her right hand had begun to shake. She laid the pencil down, and put that hand flat on the desk, beside the other, and went through it a third time with nothing in her fingers at all.

When she lifted her head, she did not look at any of them.

"I can read it."

"Then read it," said the man. His eyes were still shut.

"Aloud?"

"I've had that on a shelf ten years, a hand's breadth from my head, and I never knew a word that was in it. I'll hear it before I'm older."

She was silent a moment.

"Once," she said. "I shall read it once, aloud, as nearly as I can put a dead tongue into ours. I shall not do it twice. It is the first face only; there is more on its back, which is a list, and I have not gone into that." Her voice was low, and quite steady, and it cost her to keep it so. "It has a head, and then it begins."

She set one gloved finger in the air above the first line, an inch off the skin, as a man had taught her.

[[TIER-B: The leaf (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 1), read aloud once by Karis: "In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong" … "there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more." / "And the" [the scraped name: four or five letters' width, the first upright stroke showing] "— set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it. And this is the reason of the apparatus, and this is all of its reason, and let none who come after forget it, though the un-bound be forgotten: that they should not come again." — the plan gives the unit with an elision in its first sentence and a bracketed place for the scraped name; staged as Karis's one reading, standing at Cleon's desk under the fixed lamp, her finger an inch above the skin, with Cleon two paces back with his eyes shut, Cael at the desk's left side and Vastin on a box six strides off; the object (horn-coloured, point-written, the scraped place with its one upright stroke) is described before this line and is not described again; coordinator to supply protected text]]

Her finger had come to the foot of the first face.

She took it away, and did not turn the leaf. She stood with both hands flat on the oak, and looked at the wall of pale rock above the desk, where a pick had left its strokes.

"That is what it says," she said. "I have given it as nearly as I am able, and I shall make a copy, in our tongue, to the letter. Whoever was not here shall have it from the page." She drew a breath. "I ask one thing. I ask that nobody say it back. Not to me; not to each other, in his own words, on a stair. A thing that is said twice begins to be said differently. It is very old, and it is not ours to improve."

---

For a while nobody did anything.


