editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:41:Karis had come down the ladder last, with the flat held against her by one arm. The other hand lay in the breast of her coat, in a strip of a big man's shirt. She had not complained of it, and she had not slept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:187:They had got the old man off the wheels at the dock's corner, as they were bidden. He stood between Cael and the wall, on one leg, with his left arm across a pair of shoulders and the stick in his right hand; and so they came along the paving to the hatch, three boots abreast and a fourth that did not touch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:245:It asked of the cross-way, and of each of the black uprights, one after another, as a new dog goes along a fence. He told it no at every post. It did not sulk; it only stood where it had been stood, under his breastbone, with the patience of a thing that had been made for waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:121:She put her hand into the breast of her coat, and brought out a square of oilcloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:97:It went to the strap that crossed the breast of his coat, and rested there, in the firelight, in front of six people. Cael had seen that hand go to that strap perhaps four times in all the roads since Ostrand; he had never seen it go there when anybody was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:79:He had wanted this, and had written so, on the second night of the lamp, in three words, and had written that he did not say it. He had come to that town with a place under his breastbone which five readings had gone round and none had entered, and he had put down in a book, at a fire, that he wanted an instrument for what does not move. He had one now, resting an inch behind his eyes, as quiet as a folded rule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:339:The girl's lips had parted. She was listening now. Whatever it was had said its one word, down at the bottom of the breastbone, where nobody could overhear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:351:She was thinking of a boy on a table, under her two hands, a long while since, who had told her a thing with his breath going slow. He had stood in a ring too, and it had been chalk, and there had been a clerk. A small light had come up from behind his breastbone to the edge of his eye, as it had come just now to that child; and it had looked at him, for a count that he still knew; and then it had gone out. She had asked him whether it hurt. He had said no; he had said that it was like a wick being pinched.

