editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:229:[[TIER-B: Cleon's warning (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 10): "I'm going to pretend you didn't tell me that," he said, "for your sake, not mine. But I'll tell you one thing for free, since you can apparently take my whole trade by watching me work it for a minute." … "Everything I read is *made*. Stone, ink, seal — somebody built it, so I can see how. If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be *made* and reads it anyway — " He stopped. "Don't tell me. Don't tell anyone. Just remember I said it." — the plan gives the unit with an elision between its halves; staged as Cleon's, said at the third table after he has set the cup of water down, to the board and not to Cael's face; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:201:[[TIER-B: "Out here you count who's owed a seat," … "It's a different ledger." — the plan quotes this unit with an elision (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier C, S14) and does not pin its speaker (the packet has only "Vastin and the cups"); staged at the long table of the inn's lower room, after Vastin has remarked on the two cups kept under saucers for Karis and Oryn and Brom has said "That's so". The lines before and after are written so that either Vastin (as a thing he has worked out) or Brom (as his answer) could own it; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:101:[[TIER-B: The leaf (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 1), read aloud once by Karis: "In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong" … "there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more." / "And the" [the scraped name: four or five letters' width, the first upright stroke showing] "— set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it. And this is the reason of the apparatus, and this is all of its reason, and let none who come after forget it, though the un-bound be forgotten: that they should not come again." — the plan gives the unit with an elision in its first sentence and a bracketed place for the scraped name; staged as Karis's one reading, standing at Cleon's desk under the fixed lamp, her finger an inch above the skin, with Cleon two paces back with his eyes shut, Cael at the desk's left side and Vastin on a box six strides off; the object (horn-coloured, point-written, the scraped place with its one upright stroke) is described before this line and is not described again; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:133:[[TIER-B: Karis: "a title, not a name; the name's gone" — BOOK_MAP §9 gives this fragment of Karis's single paraphrase and not its sentence; staged as her next words at the desk, with the pencil's butt on the word at the leaf's head and then on the scraped place; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:145:[[TIER-B: *Older. Made. Us.* — BOOK_MAP §10 Tier A 3 heads this "Karis's copy and finding" and prints it after the copy; the packet sets it directly after her single paraphrase and before Cael's catalogue of the room, and pins no speaker or writer; placed here, by the packet's order. The lines before and after are written so that Karis (said, or written at the foot of her notebook's leaf) or Cael (his own reduction) could own it; coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:199:[[TIER-B: Vastin: "… the mark that is given at the kindling —" — the packet gives only the stopped end of his sentence ("Vastin reaches ('the mark that is given at the kindling —')") and BOOK_MAP §9 says that his sentence stops in the chamber; staged as said from Brom's box by the nearest bay, six strides from the desk, to the shelves and not to any person, and broken off by Karis's next line; coordinator to supply protected text]]

CH35
     1	# Chapter 35 — Lean
     2	
     3	The tall book lay open on the table's bare end, and the ninth sheet of the Stair lay beside it, foot to foot, in the round of the carried lamp.
     4	
     5	Cael had the best place in the gallery for both, as he had for everything, and the worst way up. From his knee he saw a page of the cramped brown hand, line under line, with a figure at the end of each: some clerk of the old town had set down what came in at a gate on one day, and what was paid on it. A hand's breadth off lay Karis's hard clean pencil, in rows, with the white pebbles and the black still lying where she had laid them that forenoon.
     6	
     7	The man had not begun at once. He stood with his hands behind him and his eyes shut, as he had stood on the first morning while she laid out fifty-two sheets. Karis stood on the rock where he had put her. She was a head shorter than he was, and she had come up a little on her toes without knowing it, to have her eyes where his would be.
     8	
     9	Then he opened them, and looked at the book.
    10	
    11	He looked from its foot. Cael had moved the flame before the grey head moved, and it was there. The eyes went up the brown page slowly, without a blink, and the water came into them, and they went up the sheet beside it. Then, which they had never done in eight days, they went back and forth between the two.
    12	
    13	He spoke while he looked. That was new as well. He had always looked first, and said afterward what the looking had found, in as few words as would carry it.
    14	
    15	[[TIER-B: Cleon: "A record sits … An instruction *leans* … This is a stair." — the plan quotes this speech in three pieces with elisions (packet, "The reader (291st)"; BOOK_MAP §10 Tier C S13 gives the second and third pieces); staged as Cleon's, said to Karis while his eyes go between the open tally-book (a record of loads) and the Stair's ninth sheet laid beside it foot to foot, with Cael on one knee a hand to the left holding the lamp; coordinator to supply protected text]]
    16	
    17	Nobody in the gallery moved.
    18	
    19	Brom had come up as far as the table's corner, and stopped there, with his arms hanging. Lira sat against a table's leg with her chin on her knees. On a box by the nearest bay, a long grey man had turned his head toward the two things on the table, and his hands had gone still on the ash.
    20	
    21	Karis was not looking at the sheet any longer. She had turned, on the spot where she had been put, and her eyes were going down the gallery: over the Hall's twelve, and the long table with its three rows, and the Ring's eleven beyond. She looked at them as she had once looked at a bluff from six miles off, counting.
    22	
    23	"The strata lean too."
    24	
    25	She had said it very low, and to herself.
    26	
    27	The man made a sound in his chest. It was not the grunt that he gave for a thing well answered, and it was not the other, which had been nearly a laugh. He had heard a thing that he had not thought of; Cael could tell so much from the set of his shoulders, and no more. He did not take it up. He bent again to the sheet, and one square pale finger came out over the paper, an inch above her pencil, as it went above the lines of his own page in the mornings.
    28	
    29	The finger began at the low left corner, and went up, a row at a time, and across.
    30	
    31	Cael had the light there before it, and did not have to think of where. He had been looking where that man looked for six days together, a hand to the left of him, and had taken it for a groom's knowledge of a horse. The finger came to the fifth row, and went along the signs, the white and the three black and the red, without touching one of them, and slowed as it went.
    32	
    33	[[TIER-B: "there" — the packet names this as the word at which the twelfth arrives ("the acquisition at 'there'") and gives neither its sentence nor its speaker; staged as the next thing said over the Stair's ninth sheet, at the moment when Cleon's finger comes to rest above the end of the fifth row. The paragraphs after this line are written so that Cleon or Karis could own the word. If the word falls inside Cleon's speech above, strike this marker and let Karis's "The strata lean too." follow the acquisition; coordinator to supply protected text]]
    34	
    35	It came at that word.
    36	
    37	It did not come as any of the eleven had come. Nine of them had come out of a blow, or a throw, or a bar set across a doorway. Tide had filled him like a cup under a healer's palm; and a wall had been stood in him only the evening before, as a post is stood in its hole. This was neither poured nor planted. It was turned. He had read every paper of his life from the wrong side of somebody's desk, with its head toward the person who owned it; and it was as though a hand had reached across, without a word, and turned the sheet about, so that for once it lay facing him.
    38	
    39	The fifth row leaned.
    40	
    41	Nothing on the paper had stirred; the signs stood where her pencil had put them, square and small, each in its place. But the row had a way to it now, as a field of barley has a way when the wind has gone over it and every stalk still bows a little toward where the wind went. The white sign at the head stood up straight, and all that followed it hung from it, and inclined, each toward the next. At the row's end the lean came down flat, like a foot arriving on a floor.
    42	
    43	*Goes*, it said, of that row. It said it without any voice at all; it was only there, as the weight of a stone is there in the hand that lifts it.
    44	
    45	At the tail of his eye there was something dark, propped against the table's edge with its head a little back. He did not turn his face to it, and had no need to: it had four rows cut in it, and every one of the four leaned as the fifth row did, down from a white.
    46	
    47	He put his eyes on his own knuckles, and kept them there.
    48	
    49	There was a table under that sheet, with pegs in it. There was a ring of brass in his fingers, and a desk six strides off, and a stair of dark stone that somebody had laid. The new thing inclined toward each of them in turn, mildly, like a head coming round. He did not let it look. He had been given a pair of eyes that were not his, in a room that belonged to the man they came from, and he did not know yet what they cost.
    50	
    51	It was the twelfth. He knew whose it was, and he had been a hand to its left for six days.
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	"Red," said the rough voice. "At that one. And at that. He's finished, at those; he begins again after."
    56	
    57	It was said to the chair by the wall. The finger had gone back to the head of the sheet, and it was coming down the rows now, stopping above each place where a run ended. At every stop Karis laid a red square of broken tile below her pencil, between one sign and its neighbour.
    58	
    59	She had asked for his joins, and he was giving them to her; he could not have done it with a pebble five days ago. He had needed to say aloud, to somebody who stood in his boots, what he had stood in them and seen for thirty years; and it seemed that the saying had loosened the rest.
    60	
    61	"Five," she said, of a group. Her voice shook, and she let it. "That is one word. It is five signs long, and it is one word, and I have four of its letters."
    62	
    63	"Then you've a book to lay it against."
    64	
    65	She was at the tall book before he had done. Cael held the flame for her, a hand to the left, and watched her pencil go down the old clerk's column, hunting a word of five with those four in their places. She found it in the time that it takes to draw a long breath; and she found it again, twelve lines lower, and only then did she write.
    66	
    67	"Fifteen."
    68	
    69	Cael knelt, and kept the light where it was wanted, and thought about what he had.
    70	
    71	It leaned at the tall book, when her hand turned a leaf. *Sits*, it would have said of that page, if he had let it: he could feel the word waiting, flat and level, like a board laid on two trestles. It leaned at the lamp-ring, and at the joint of the table's leg, a foot from his knee, where one peg did the work and another had been put in for show. He told it no each time. It was an easier thing to refuse than Shadow, which pulled, or the wall, which asked; this one only offered, like a clerk holding out a sheet that need not be taken.
    72	
    73	He understood the lamp now. He had put it low and to the left for six days because he was told to, and had moved it ahead of the word because the eyes always went one way. They went one way because the thing itself did. A made thing was looked at from where it stood upon the ground, toward where it was going, and a light set anywhere else lay across the grain of it.
    74	
    75	That was a small matter, and he was glad to have it; it was the only part of the business that he felt sure he had come by honestly.
    76	
    77	There was the other part.
    78	
    79	He had wanted this, and had written so, on the second night of the lamp, in three words, and had written that he did not say it. He had come to that town with a place under his breastbone which five readings had gone round and none had entered, and he had put down in a book, at a fire, that he wanted an instrument for what does not move. He had one now, resting an inch behind his eyes, as quiet as a folded rule.
    80	
    81	He looked at that squarely, with his breath going long and the flame standing up.
    82	
    83	It would take no time; he need not so much as shift his knee. He had only to let the thing come round, as it kept trying to come round to a table's leg, and turn it instead upon the one place in himself that he had never been able to read.
    84	
    85	He did not do it.
    86	
    87	There were two reasons, and he made himself set them in order, since a wish is quick and a reason has to be fetched. The man who owned this had told a gallery what it was not for, five days ago, in the voice of a carter giving his rate. It did not work on a man. And a healer had put an order on that place, on a plank, a long way east: *Write it down and stop touching it.* He had read her order once already for the leave that he wanted. He had gone round the place with what he had, at a waypost, and had called it obedience, since he had not gone in. He had been ashamed enough of that to write it, and to owe her the telling.
    88	
    89	He would not read her twice. He would be at her mill inside of eight days, and she could be asked.
    90	
    91	*Not until she's heard it*, he said, to the thing behind his eyes; and it lay down, having only ever offered.
    92	
    93	"Sixteen," said Karis. She had dust on her cheek, and her left hand had come up out of her lap and was holding the tall book's leaf flat, on its bound palm, as though it had forgotten that it was hurt. "It is a score. It is the old word for twenty, and there are a hundred of them on this page."
    94	
    95	"He counted by the score, then," said Brom, from the table's corner. "Like a drover."
    96	
    97	"I am not saying how he counted. I am saying what I can spell."
    98	
    99	The third thing was harder than both, and it was not in him at all. It stood at the table's end with its hands in the small of its back.
   100	
   101	A man had shown a stranger the inside of his trade, for the first time in thirty years, because she had asked him honestly for something that he could not hand her. And the boy who held his lamp had come away with a piece of it. He had not reached. He never did reach. But he would kneel at that elbow tomorrow, and the day after, with the man's own way of looking folded behind his eyes; and each time that the finger went along a row, he would be able to see where it was going before it went.
   102	
   103	That was not a thing that had been taken once, in an alley, and now sat idle. It would be taken again every hour, for as long as the lamp was his.
   104	
   105	"Seventeen," said Karis, when the flame had begun to smoke. She said it as a person says the name of a town that she has walked to.
   106	
   107	"Of twenty-three," said the man.
   108	
   109	"Yes."
   110	
   111	"Two days."
   112	
   113	"I know what I have. I did not know this morning that I should have it so quickly." She looked up at him, from the chair. "You have never shown that to anybody."
   114	
   115	"Nobody wanted his joins."
   116	
   117	He went to his desk to write the card. A long way off, at the stair's foot, something small and flat was struck once, like a spoon on a cup.
   118	
   119	---
   120	
   121	The old man had gone to the stair a quarter of an hour before the bell, by himself.
   122	
   123	He had said that he would. A person who must climb as he had to climb did well to begin early. He had gone off down the gallery on the stick, hand over hand along the black uprights, with a civil refusal for each of the three who offered an arm. He had left the tarred case leaning at the foot of the first flight. Cael heard him before he saw the place: a slow scrape, and a breath, and a scrape, somewhere up in the dark of the first flight.
   124	
   125	"He's on the first landing," said Lira. "On his seat, backward. I'd not have thought he could."
   126	
   127	She had the lamp, and she had set her foot on the bottom tread. Brom was behind Cael, under his box, and Karis behind Brom, with her notebook shut against her chest and seventeen letters in it.
   128	
   129	Cael went up two steps, until his mouth was at the height of her ear.
   130	
   131	"Twelfth. His. Lean."
   132	
   133	He had said it under the scraping. The old man was nine steps above them, with his back to the climb, making noise enough for three; they used the voices that a road had taught them, all the same.
   134	
   135	The lamp did not move; he would have known it if it had, having become a judge of such things. Only her head came round, slowly, and her eyes went over his face in that brown light as they went over a page of his book.
   136	
   137	"From him," she said, hardly above a breath. "Down there, with the pebbles. He never laid a hand on anybody."
   138	
   139	"He showed her how he sees it. I was a hand off."
   140	
   141	"When?"
   142	
   143	"The fifth row. You were sitting against the table."
   144	
   145	"I thought you'd gone white. I thought it was your knee." She looked up the stair, at the dark that was scraping, and down again. "Does it ask? Like the other one?"
   146	
   147	"It doesn't ask. It leans. It's leaning at this stair now, and I'm not letting it."
   148	
   149	Brom had heard, as he heard everything on a stair, having nothing to do there but carry. He let his box down upon the first tread, by his knees, and sat on it.
   150	
   151	"That's two in two days," he said. "You'll want a bigger book."
   152	
   153	"I'm going back down. I'm going to tell him."
   154	
   155	Nobody answered at once. Above them a boot's heel knocked on stone, and there was a pause, and a breath.
   156	
   157	"You've one up there that you couldn't tell," said Lira. She did not say it unkindly; she had read two lines in a book, the night before, and had read them twice. "You wrote that you can't tell the taking without the rest, and the rest isn't yours. It's six people's."
   158	
   159	"It is. Three of them are on this stair."
   160	
   161	He had been turning it over since the fifth row, and he gave it to them as it stood, with the weak places showing.
   162	
   163	"That one came once, and it's shut, and I've not touched it. To tell him that much, I'd have to tell him all of it, and I can't take it back out of him afterward. This one's different, and it's not because I think better of the man. I'm at his elbow every day. He says a word and I'm there before it, and from tomorrow I'd be there because I can see what he sees. He made it a term that nobody tells him what a thing is before he's looked. He's looked for a week at a lamp, and it's not a lamp."
   164	
   165	"He does not want to be told things," said Karis.
   166	
   167	She had come up beside Brom's knee. Her face was white and tired, and she was holding the notebook as she held anything that she meant to keep.
   168	
   169	"He wants to look. He said so at a counter, before he would let me say one word of what I thought. But I do not think that he would thank us for letting him go on looking beside a thing that he had not been shown." She drew a breath. "I shall sit in that chair tomorrow. I should not care to sit there knowing what he does not. He has given me his joins."
   170	
   171	"Seln told a clerk the truth in a yard," said Brom. "It cost him his face. He said that it was the only way he'd found that stayed right afterward." He looked at his hands. "The fellow's not a counter. He's kept all that for thirty years with never a ticket on a shelf. Tell him what's his. I'd not tell him ours."
   172	
   173	"I'll tell him how this one came. No more than that."
   174	
   175	"Then that's three," said Lira. "You'll have the other two to face. One's on a roof, and one's got a mule." She shifted the ring to her other fingers and back again. "Go on. I'll hold his light at the top till he comes for it. Leave the box."
   176	
   177	He slid the beckets off. Brom shifted along, and laid one forearm across the lid of the third box, with a purse in it under five thumbs, as a man lays his arm along a gate.
   178	
   179	"I've a hand on it," he said.
   180	
   181	Cael went back along the cross-way, into the middle gallery, with no lamp at all.
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	It was a hundred and ten strides, and he walked them toward one small light.
   186	
   187	The desk's lamp burned at the far end, on its iron arm, as it had burned on the first morning; and for most of the way it showed him nothing but itself. The shelves went by on either hand as walls go by in a dream. The new thing leaned at every bay. He kept his eyes on the flame, and counted.
   188	
   189	The man was not at his desk. He stood at the third table, in the spill of that good light, with the black plank tipped toward him on its foot and the reed in his fingers. He was looking at its first row, from below, and he did not turn at the sound of boots.
   190	
   191	"You've a quarter of an hour, and you've left your light," he said. "If she's forgotten a book, it'll keep."
   192	
   193	"It's not a book."
   194	
   195	Cael had stopped at the table's end. He had never stood in front of that man without a ring in his fingers, and his hands hung strangely; he put them at his sides.
   196	
   197	"You said no names, and I'll give you none. You said that nobody was to tell you what a thing was until you'd looked. You've looked. There's a thing about the hand that's held your lamp, and I'd sooner you had it from me tonight than found it out tomorrow."
   198	
   199	The reed had stopped, and the grey head did not come round.
   200	
   201	He told it in the order in which he had it, which was the order in which it would have to be written: what the thing stood beside, and whose it had been, and how much of it he had; and then, since that was what the man would want, how it had come. He said that he had no Path, and that a plate had told him so. He said that pieces of other people's came to him all the same, when he stood near somebody who was using what he had in earnest. He did not reach for them, he said, and could not stop them; and there had been eleven that morning.
   202	
   203	"There's a twelfth. It came an hour ago, at the fifth row, when your finger stopped. It's a piece of yours. I can see which way a run goes. I looked at that row, and at your board, and I've looked at nothing since."
   204	
   205	"You did what."
   206	
   207	It was not asked; it was said flat, to the oak, as a man says the weight of a thing that has been set down on his foot.
   208	
   209	"I can see the lean. I couldn't this morning."
   210	
   211	The plank went back against the table's edge. He set it there with both hands, slowly, as he would have set down a full jar; and still he had not turned.
   212	
   213	"That's not in the classification system. That's not *supposed* to be possible. That's — "
   214	
   215	His voice had risen. Cael had not heard it rise in eight days, for a dray or a cut hand or a lamp that moved; and it had gone up now like a clerk's who has turned a leaf and found a figure where no figure can be.
   216	
   217	[[TIER-B: "No," … "No. I'll not say that one. Not down here. Not to you." — the plan quotes this unit with an elision (BOOK_MAP §10 Tier C S13; the packet gives "No. I'll not say that one."); staged as Cleon's, breaking off his own sentence at the board, with his back still to Cael; coordinator to supply protected text]]
   218	
   219	Cael did not ask him for the word.
   220	
   221	He could not have named it. He knew only that the man had it, whole, ready behind his teeth, and had judged it too heavy a thing to hand across a table to a boy; and he found that he would rather be spared a word by that man than be given one by most.
   222	
   223	The square hands had opened on the table's edge, and shut. Then the man walked to his desk, with one hand before him, by habit. He reached up to the shelf under the iron arm. He took down the cup, and the jar, and poured. He carried the cup back along the tables, and set it on the bare corner of the third, a long way from any paper, an inch from Cael's left hand.
   224	
   225	He did not say what it was for; it was water, and it was the first thing that he had ever given to the person who held his lamp that was not an order.
   226	
